      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pickensville is the oldest town in Pickens County, with a post office established in 1823.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/pickensville/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "It was initially incorporated in 1839 and briefly served as the first county seat of Pickens County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pickensville%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Pickensville’s historic Methodist Church and Rosenwald School are listed on the Alabama Register of Landmarks and Heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/pickensville/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A reproduction of a mid‑nineteenth‑century plantation home in Pickensville displays the history of the Tombigbee River and Waterway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/pickensville/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The steam‑driven sternwheeler Snagboat Montgomery, a National Historic Landmark, is moored by the river near Pickensville’s center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/pickensville/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
